A||N||14||pherder||Possible source for headers for V8 swap (?)||||1||
Z||000000||pherder||07-22-2000||03:32 PM||||While looking thru a copy of JC Whitney, I found a listing for 92-96 LT1 Corvette headers, coated, for $279.95.<P>Took a trip to a car lot and looked at the exhaust manifolds on a 95 Corvette and they looked like a set of headers might work.<P>So I ordered a set.  They came in today.<P> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=208525&a=6248154" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=208525&a=6248154</A>  has the pictures of the headers installed on my TPI engine.<P>They turn in a little more then I want but I believe they will work.  I will know for sure when I get the engine mounted on a cradle with the transaxle and axles all mounted up.<P>------------------<BR>Paul, Sedgwick, KS<BR>88 Coupe, 88 Formula, 88 GT<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 07-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||buds||07-24-2000||07:06 AM||||I bought block hugger headers for my swap,they won't fit.the back header falls right in line with passenger side axle.please post if they will fit for sure.||||reg||9
Z||000002||buds||07-24-2000||09:10 AM||||After loking at your picture they look the same as my block huggers.Be sure to check that they are out of the way of the passenger side axle...||||reg||3
Z||000003||batboy||07-24-2000||03:20 PM||||Paul, you will be my hero if you find a decent pair of V8 headers that fit.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||07-24-2000||06:31 PM||||Buds,<P>I had a set of block huggers to "try" on my earlier attempt.  They did like they are named ... hugged the block.  I guess you found out like I did that there wasn't any way they would work without a lot of cutting/rewelding.<P>BTW, I got a post back from Archie that he has already used a set of these PaceSetter headers on a swap.  He prefers the Sanderson CC90s since there is more room to angle the down pipe to clear the axle but he wrote these PaceSetters will work.<P>All else fails, I will get a set of Sanderson CC90s and use the Sandersons for the rear (axle side) and the PaceSetters for the A/C side.  That way I can have two V8 Fieros with headers!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The Sanderson headers are shown here:<P> <A HREF="http://www.centuryperformance.com/sanderson20.htm#CC90" TARGET=_blank>http://www.centuryperformance.com/sanderson20.htm#CC90</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>Paul, Sedgwick, KS<BR>88 Coupe, 88 Formula, 88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||07-24-2000||07:13 PM||||<BR>I was always under the impression that the main benefit of using headers was to help the exhaust flow more efficiently. Two of the ways that this is accomplished is by using "tuned" equal length primary tubes for each cylinder and by having a collector of proper dimensions on each bank of cylinders. These "shorty" headers don't have primary tubes that are even close to being tuned or equal length and there is no collector at all to speak of. Is there any real benefit to using them?<P>Do any of you "old" guys remember the cast iron <B>ram's head</B> exhaust manifolds used on older (60's) V8 Chevys? They were supposed to flow better than the usual OEM cast iron manifolds. Would a set of those work at all in a V8 Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000006||pherder||07-24-2000||07:28 PM||||Patrick,<P>To answer your second question ... yes I remember the Rams head manifold and that is what is supplied by V8Archie when he sells a complete exhaust system.  <P>There is one thing you did not mention when compairing the shorty headers with the CAST IRON exhaust manifolds ... weight and that is the main reason I am going with the shorty headers (along with the aluminum heads, manifold, etc.)<P>You can have "tuned headers" in a Fiero as Eric Marsh has in his 88 Fiero (picture "borrowed" from his site)<P> <IMG SRC="http://rodnclassics.com/emarsh/images/engine_rear.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Paul, Sedgwick, KS<BR>88 Coupe, 88 Formula, 88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||07-24-2000||07:44 PM||||Patrick, you're right about the shorty headers not flowing quite as well as tuned headers. But, the do flow much better than the factory log manifolds. I remember the Ram's Horn manifolds, in fact, I have a couple of pair of them buried out in the shed. I've had to use them on a couple of my streetrods, so my mechanic friend saves them back for me when he runs across them super cheap. They do flow a little better than the regular log manifolds, but not as well as the shorties. Corvette manifolds are also supposed to flow better than the stock manifolds. Kind of like a factory header.<P>I just went out and measured and eyeballed the ram's horn manifolds on my 72 truck (most 67-72 Chevy trucks used them). Ok, there are two styles of those manifolds. First style dumps straight down. On an auto Fiero, the speed sensor is right smack dab in the way. The other style angles the dump at a 45 degree towards the rear. If you use a driver's side manifold that dumped to the passenger side (talking about the manifold closest to the trunk) of a V8 Fiero, it might work with a 180 degree mandrel bend, but it looks tight, very tight.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||07-24-2000||07:45 PM||||<BR>Ah yes, the weight factor. That goes without saying Paul. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Those are some headers that Eric has. I remember seeing them pictured (or linked to) here before. They are really a work of art. It would seem be be a pity to hide them away in some engine compartment. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000009||pherder||07-24-2000||07:51 PM||||What do you mean "hide away"????<P>When his engine cover is opened, all you can see is those headers!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>"OH, there is an engine in there too??"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>AND if the sun is just right, they will blind you!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>AND Batboy, if you noticed ... he is running an automatic.  They would fit your Batmobile!<P><P>------------------<BR>Paul, Sedgwick, KS<BR>88 Coupe, 88 Formula, 88 GT||||reg||5
Z||000010||Patrick||07-24-2000||07:51 PM||||<BR>Batboy, I'm not surprised you're familiar with the <B>ram's head</B> exhaust manifold. You're about the same age as me. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000011||batboy||07-24-2000||09:59 PM||||Well, I don't mean to nitpick, but I thought maybe I was wrong calling them "ram's horn" manifolds instead of "ram's head", but after looking it up in my Chevrolet Power book, they are calling them ram's horn also. The book also says they flow well for cast iron manifolds, especially the early Vette ones with 2-1/2" outlets. I think most of the other cast iron manifolds have 1-7/8" outlets (?). The other Vette manifold I referred to in my earlier post is the 86-90 Corvette stainless tubular manifold, but I have no idea if they will work. From the pic in the book, it don't look like it, but who knows? Another option is the 82-87 Camaro IROC-Z manifold with 2-1/4" outlets used in the 350 performance package (I have part numbers). I might have to look into these some more since they will clear the raised rocker cover rails on HO aluminum heads. Just more food for thought.||||reg||1
Z||000012||pherder||07-24-2000||10:06 PM||||You want Cast Iron Corvette Exhaust Manifolds (Ram Horn) ???<P>Go to this URL:<P> <A HREF="http://www.castheads.com/exhaust/exhaust.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.castheads.com/exhaust/exhaust.htm</A> <P>They even will port them for better flow!!!  AND they will sell you the piping to connect them up!<P><P>------------------<BR>Paul, Sedgwick, KS<BR>88 Coupe, 88 Formula, 88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||07-24-2000||11:25 PM||||Wow! Paul, those manifolds must be gold plated or something...boy, are they proud of those things. I didn't even bother bookmarking that site...too expensive. I gotta start looking for those 2.5" Vette Ram's Horn manifolds at the swap meets. Lots of wrecked IROC-Z Camaros around here...kids tear 'em up pretty quick. Gotta look for those manifolds too, or at least get a pic and dimensions.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Patrick||07-25-2000||12:10 AM||||<BR>Ram's <I>head</I> or ram's <I>horn</I>, whatever, I was close. Hey, it's only been a decade or two since I've had a reason to mention them to anyone. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>One advantage of using the cast iron exhaust manifolds is that at least they'll never rust out like headers do. They're also quieter, although I doubt any of us would be TOO concerned about that. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
